The Sheryl Lee Ralph -produced television special &#x201C;Until Everyone Is Seen&#x201D; will premiere on World AIDS Day on Dec. 1 on Hulu.

&#x201C;Until Everyone Is Seen&#x201D; chronicles efforts to support unhoused men and women living on the streets in major cities, including people with HIV and those who could benefit from HIV prevention measures.

Filmed in Atlanta, the special highlights the work of D&#xE1;zon Dixon Diallo, who founded SisterLove, Inc. in 1989 as the first women&#x2019;s HIV, sexual and reproductive justice organization in the southeastern United States, and Apostle Will Francis, whose ministry, The HUB, brings meals, mobile showers, laundry services and care directly to Atlanta&#x2019;s unhoused communities. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Gwyneth Paltrow's 'Goop Guide' Show Coming to Netflix and Spotify, in Which She Explains 'How to Live Goop' in 'All the Ways That Can Be Defined' How Netflix‘s Animated 'Steps’ Reimagines the Cinderella Story Through the Eyes of Her Asian Stepsisters and Finds a New Kind of Happily Ever After

&#x201C;Until we see everyone, we see no one,&#x201D; Ralph, who produces alongside her nonprofit The DIVA Foundation, said in a statement on Tuesday. &#x201C;Every day in Atlanta, angels like D&#xE1;zon and Apostle Francis show up for people the world has learned to walk past. This television special asks all of us to stop, to look and to see them.&#x201D;

The special, directed by Zeberiah Newman and presented with ABC Owned Television Stations and ViiV Healthcare, is the latest chapter in the &#x201C;Abbott Elementary&#x201D; star&#x2019;s more than four-decade work supporting and fighting for those with HIV and AIDS. The franchise began in 2023 with &#x201C;Unexpected,&#x201D; which followed two young mothers who turned their own HIV diagnoses into a network of support for newly diagnosed women across the rural South. &#x201C;Living Proof&#x201D; carried that story into grassroots organizing, celebrating Black women in Atlanta building sisterhood and thriving with HIV. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;Unexpected&#x201D; earned two Daytime Emmy nominations, for outstanding daytime special and outstanding original song. &#x201C;Living Proof&#x201D; earned 2026 nominations for the Daytime Emmy for outstanding daytime special and the GLAAD Media Award for outstanding TV journalism, long form. Both specials are featured in Hulu&#x2019;s Black Stories Always hub.

&#x201C;People living with HIV have always been at the heart of the response to this epidemic, and community-led care remains one of the most powerful drivers of progress,&#x201D; said Randevyn Pi&#xE9;rre, head of U.S. external affairs at ViiV Healthcare. &#x201C;At ViiV Healthcare, we are committed to supporting the communities most impacted by HIV, elevating the voices and experiences that are too often overlooked and helping ensure everyone can access the care and support they need. The stories in this television special deserve to be heard, and we&#x2019;re proud to support &#x2018;Until Everyone Is Seen&#x2019; alongside Sheryl Lee Ralph and The DIVA Foundation.&#x201D;

Executive producers include Ralph, Newman, Michiel Thomas and Norman Lee, with production by Zables & Associates and Shivelight, LLC.

